---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

export const frontmatter = {
  title: 'Kimi Delta Attention, Illustrated: one equation, six architectures, and a 75% smaller cache',
  pubDate: '2026-08-09',
};
---

<BlogPostLayout frontmatter={frontmatter}>
  <p class="mb-6 text-gray-700">
    Attention has a bill that comes due at long context: every token you have already read has to be
    kept around, forever, in case a later token wants to look at it. <b>Kimi Linear</b> is the first
    hybrid linear-attention architecture its authors claim beats full attention outright — not
    "close enough for the speedup", but better, on short context as well as long. At its centre is
    <b>Kimi Delta Attention</b>, and KDA turns out to differ from its predecessor by exactly one
    thing: a knob that used to be a single number became a vector.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-6 text-gray-700">
    This post builds that equation up one piece at a time, with figures you can poke at — every one
    of which runs the real recurrence in your browser rather than replaying a recording. Then it
    reports what happened when I trained the whole family myself on two RTX 3090s, including the
    parts that did not reproduce and the four bugs that made everything look fine while being
    completely wrong. If you know what attention and a training loop are, you have enough to follow along.
  </p>

  <div class="mb-6 p-4 rounded border bg-blue-50 text-blue-900">
    <b>TL;DR.</b> Linear attention replaces the KV cache with one fixed-size matrix — an associative
    memory. That memory <i>interferes</i> with itself, which the <b>delta rule</b> fixes by writing the
    prediction error instead of the value, and which <b>gating</b> further fixes by letting old entries
    fade. Gated DeltaNet gives each attention head one forgetting rate; <b>KDA gives every channel its
    own</b>. That single change is the paper's contribution, and it also lets the decay act as a learned
    positional encoding, which is why the full-attention layers in Kimi Linear have no positional
    encoding at all.
  </div>

  <nav class="mb-6 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 text-gray-700 text-sm md:text-base">
    <b class="block mb-1">Contents</b>
    <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-0.5">
      <li><a href="#bill" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">The bill comes due</a></li>
      <li><a href="#parentheses" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">Moving the parentheses</a></li>
      <li><a href="#interference" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">The memory interferes with itself</a></li>
      <li><a href="#delta" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">Attention as online learning</a></li>
      <li><a href="#forgetting" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">Learning to forget</a></li>
      <li><a href="#kda" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">KDA: one dial per channel</a></li>
      <li><a href="#position" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">The decay is a positional encoding</a></li>
      <li><a href="#fast" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">Making it fast</a></li>
      <li><a href="#hybrid" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">The hybrid</a></li>
      <li><a href="#results" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">Does it hold up?</a></li>
      <li><a href="#wrong" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">Four things I got wrong</a></li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <!-- ============================================================ 1 -->
  <section id="bill" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">1. The bill comes due</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Softmax attention has a property that is easy to admire and expensive to keep: it never
      compresses anything. To produce the next token it re-reads every previous key and value in
      full. That is why it is so good at exact recall, and it is also the entire problem. Serving a
      one-million-token context means holding a million keys and a million values, per layer, in
      memory that grows linearly and is read back on <em>every single generated token</em>.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Drag the context length below and watch the number that a serving system actually has to fit in
      GPU memory.
    </p>

    <div data-widget="cache-growth"></div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      There are two costs hiding here and they behave differently. Prefill — reading the prompt — is
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">O(T²)</span> compute, painful but parallel. Decoding is
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">O(T)</span> memory traffic per token, and memory traffic is the
      thing modern GPUs are starved for. Long-context decoding is not compute-bound; it is bound by
      dragging that cache across the memory bus once per token.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ 2 -->
  <section id="parentheses" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">2. Moving the parentheses</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Here is the trick the whole field is built on. Strip the softmax out of attention and what
      remains is three matrices multiplied together:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 font-mono text-sm md:text-base text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      O = (Q Kᵀ) V
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Matrix multiplication is associative, so you may equally compute
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">Q (Kᵀ V)</span>. Same answer. Wildly different cost. The first
      order builds a <span class="font-mono text-sm">T×T</span> object — every token against every
      other token. The second builds a <span class="font-mono text-sm">d×d</span> object that does not
      grow with the sequence at all. The softmax is exactly what prevents this regrouping, because
      it sits between <span class="font-mono text-sm">QKᵀ</span> and <span class="font-mono text-sm">V</span>
      and is not linear.
    </p>

    <div data-widget="associativity"></div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Note the crossover in that figure: below roughly <span class="font-mono text-sm">T ≈ 3d</span>,
      quadratic attention is the <em>cheaper</em> option. Linear attention is not a free lunch, it is
      a different asymptote — and one you only start collecting on at long context.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Written causally, that <span class="font-mono text-sm">d×d</span> object becomes a state you
      carry forward one token at a time:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 font-mono text-sm md:text-base text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      S<sub>t</sub> = S<sub>t−1</sub> + k<sub>t</sub> v<sub>t</sub>ᵀ &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; o<sub>t</sub> = S<sub>t</sub>ᵀ q<sub>t</sub>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      The KV cache is gone. In its place is one matrix per head, the same size at token 10 as at token
      10 million. This is <b>linear attention</b> (Katharopoulos et al., 2020), and if it simply worked
      we would all be using it. It does not, and the reason is worth seeing directly.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ 3 -->
  <section id="interference" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">3. The memory interferes with itself</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      That state has a name outside deep learning: it is an <b>associative memory</b>, the same object
      Hopfield networks and "fast weights" are built from. You write pairs into it with an outer
      product and read them back by multiplying with a key. So what happens when you write several?
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Store pairs <span class="font-mono text-sm">(k₁,v₁) … (kₙ,vₙ)</span>, then query with
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">k<sub>j</sub></span>:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 font-mono text-sm text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      Sᵀk<sub>j</sub> = ‖k<sub>j</sub>‖² v<sub>j</sub> &nbsp;+&nbsp; Σ<sub>i≠j</sub> (k<sub>i</sub>·k<sub>j</sub>) v<sub>i</sub><br/>
      <span class="text-gray-500">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;the value you wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;everything else, leaking</span>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      The second term vanishes only if the keys are mutually orthogonal — and a
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">d</span>-dimensional space holds at most
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">d</span> orthogonal directions. Past that, every write corrupts
      the others, permanently, because nothing in this rule ever erases. Add pairs in the figure below
      and watch the answer degrade.
    </p>

    <div data-widget="interference"></div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Leave that checkbox unticked for now — we are about to earn it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ 4 -->
  <section id="delta" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">4. Attention as online learning</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      The most useful reframing in this literature: treat the state not as a container but as a
      <em>model being trained during the forward pass</em>. Each token is one training example, and the
      update rule is one optimiser step. What differs between architectures is the loss they descend.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Plain linear attention descends
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">ℒ = −⟨Sᵀk<sub>t</sub>, v<sub>t</sub>⟩</span>. That objective is
      unbounded — it rewards making the response to <span class="font-mono text-sm">k<sub>t</sub></span>
      ever larger, with no notion of "enough" and no criterion for what to erase. Hence the pile-up.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      DeltaNet descends something with a floor:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 font-mono text-sm md:text-base text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      ℒ<sub>t</sub>(S) = ½ ‖ Sᵀk<sub>t</sub> − v<sub>t</sub> ‖²
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      One gradient step with learning rate <span class="font-mono text-sm">β<sub>t</sub></span> gives
      the <b>delta rule</b>:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 font-mono text-sm md:text-base text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      S<sub>t</sub> = S<sub>t−1</sub> + β<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub> ( v<sub>t</sub> − S<sub>t−1</sub>ᵀ k<sub>t</sub> )ᵀ
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Read the parenthesis: <span class="font-mono text-sm">S<sub>t−1</sub>ᵀk<sub>t</sub></span> is what
      the memory <em>already answers</em> for this key. The rule writes the <b>error</b>, not the value.
      Write a key twice and the second write corrects the first instead of stacking on top of it. Go
      back and tick the checkbox in the figure above — the retrieval error collapses.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      And <span class="font-mono text-sm">β<sub>t</sub></span> is a genuine learning rate: 0 writes
      nothing, 1 overwrites exactly, in between converges geometrically.
    </p>

    <div data-widget="beta"></div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ 5 -->
  <section id="forgetting" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">5. Learning to forget</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      The delta rule stops keys from corrupting each other, but it still never forgets. A fact written
      at token 5 is as vivid at token 500,000 as the day it arrived, competing for the same finite
      capacity. <b>Gated DeltaNet</b> adds a decay
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">α<sub>t</sub> ∈ [0,1]</span> — weight decay on the fast weights:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 font-mono text-sm md:text-base text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      S<sub>t</sub> = (I − β<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub>ᵀ) <b>α<sub>t</sub></b> S<sub>t−1</sub> + β<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub> v<sub>t</sub>ᵀ
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Now the model can choose a memory lifespan. But look closely at what
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">α<sub>t</sub></span> is: <b>one number per head, per step</b>.
      It multiplies the entire state. Everything the head knows fades at the same rate, whether it is
      the subject of the sentence or a stray comma.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ 6 -->
  <section id="kda" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">6. KDA: one dial per channel</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Here is the paper's contribution, in full:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border-2 border-blue-200 bg-blue-50 font-mono text-sm md:text-base text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      S<sub>t</sub> = (I − β<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub>ᵀ) <b class="text-blue-700">Diag(α<sub>t</sub>)</b> S<sub>t−1</sub> + β<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub> v<sub>t</sub>ᵀ
      <span class="block mt-2 text-xs text-gray-500 font-sans">Kimi Delta Attention, Eq. 1 — α<sub>t</sub> is now a vector, one entry per key channel</span>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      The scalar became a diagonal matrix. Instead of one forgetting rate per head there are 128 — one
      per channel of the state — each computed from the current token. The head can hold a name in
      some channels while letting punctuation wash through others.
    </p>

    <div data-widget="gate-granularity"></div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      What makes this satisfying is how little of the surrounding machinery it disturbs. Take one
      recurrence, give it two switches — <em>how fine-grained is the decay</em> and <em>does the write
      correct itself</em> — and six named architectures fall out as settings:
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-5">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b-2 border-gray-300 text-left">
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Decay</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Delta rule</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Update</th>
            <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Is</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="font-mono text-xs md:text-sm">
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-2 pr-4">none</td><td class="pr-4">off</td><td class="pr-4">S + k vᵀ</td><td class="font-sans">Linear Attention</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-2 pr-4">scalar</td><td class="pr-4">off</td><td class="pr-4">α S + β k vᵀ</td><td class="font-sans">Mamba2</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-2 pr-4">channel</td><td class="pr-4">off</td><td class="pr-4">Diag(α) S + k vᵀ</td><td class="font-sans">GLA</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-2 pr-4">none</td><td class="pr-4">on</td><td class="pr-4">(I − βkkᵀ) S + β k vᵀ</td><td class="font-sans">DeltaNet</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-2 pr-4">scalar</td><td class="pr-4">on</td><td class="pr-4">(I − βkkᵀ) α S + β k vᵀ</td><td class="font-sans">Gated DeltaNet</td></tr>
          <tr class="bg-blue-50 font-semibold"><td class="py-2 pr-4">channel</td><td class="pr-4">on</td><td class="pr-4">(I − βkkᵀ) Diag(α) S + β k vᵀ</td><td class="font-sans">KDA</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      This table is not a mnemonic — in the companion repository it is a
      <a href="https://github.com/eitamar-saraf/kda" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">test</a>.
      One kernel with two flags is asserted, to floating-point tolerance, to reproduce six independent
      transcriptions of the paper's Table 7, and separately to match the Triton kernels the Kimi team
      released. Broadcasting a scalar gate across all channels turns KDA back into Gated DeltaNet
      exactly; setting the decay to 1 turns it into DeltaNet.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ 7 -->
  <section id="position" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">7. The decay is a positional encoding</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      This is the most elegant observation in the paper and it is easy to miss. Unroll the recurrence
      and the contribution of token <span class="font-mono text-sm">i</span> to the state at token
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">t</span> carries the factor
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">∏<sub>j=i+1</sub><sup>t</sup> α<sub>j</sub></span> — a product of
      per-channel numbers that depends only on how far apart they are.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Compare RoPE, which multiplies by
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">∏ R<sub>j</sub></span>, a product of per-2-dimension
      <em>rotations</em> whose frequencies are fixed by hand. Both encode distance as a product of
      per-channel factors. RoPE's are periodic and chosen in advance; KDA's are monotone and learned.
    </p>

    <div data-widget="decay-as-position"></div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      This is why Kimi Linear applies <b>no positional encoding at all</b> to its full-attention
      layers. Position is delegated entirely to the KDA layers, which handle it dynamically, and the
      global layers are left free to be pure content-based retrieval. It also sidesteps the usual
      long-context headache of stretching RoPE's fixed frequencies past the lengths they were trained on.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ 8 -->
  <section id="fast" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">8. Making it fast</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Everything above is a sequential loop, which is the worst possible shape for a GPU: thousands of
      tiny dependent matrix updates, none big enough to fill a tensor core. The fix is to process the
      sequence in chunks of <span class="font-mono text-sm">C</span> = 64. Inside a chunk everything
      becomes a few dense matmuls; only chunk-to-chunk state passing stays sequential.
    </p>

    <div data-widget="chunkwise"></div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Two details in that derivation are worth pulling out, because they are where KDA earns its speed.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      <b>Binding both low-rank vectors to k.</b> Multiply out Eq. 1 and KDA's transition matrix is
    </p>
    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 font-mono text-sm text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      (I − β<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub>ᵀ) Diag(α<sub>t</sub>) &nbsp;=&nbsp; Diag(α<sub>t</sub>) − (β<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub>)(α<sub>t</sub> ⊙ k<sub>t</sub>)ᵀ
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      which is an instance of the general <em>diagonal-plus-low-rank</em> form
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">D − a bᵀ</span>. The general version carries two
      <em>independent</em> vectors <span class="font-mono text-sm">a</span> and
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">b</span>; in KDA both are the same key up to scaling —
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">a = β<sub>t</sub>k<sub>t</sub></span>,
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">b = α<sub>t</sub> ⊙ k<sub>t</sub></span> — because that is exactly
      what makes it a <em>delta</em> rule rather than an arbitrary rank-1 update. That tie cuts the
      second-level chunk matmuls from four to two and removes three more: close to 2× kernel
      throughput, obtained for free from a constraint that was already there for other reasons.
      (I have this reduction as a test rather than a claim — writing it caught two mistakes in my
      own DPLR reference before it caught anything about the kernels.)
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      <b>Never form 1/Γ.</b> The chunkwise algebra contains a division by the cumulative decay. Over 64
      tokens with α ≈ 0.8 that reciprocal reaches 10⁶, and in float16 — whose largest finite value is
      65504 — it simply becomes infinity. I measured this: computing that matrix the naive way in fp16
      produced <b>834 non-finite entries out of 4096</b>. The fix is not more precision, it is
      refactoring. Every place the ratio appears it is a <em>pairwise</em> ratio
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">γ<sub>r</sub>/γ<sub>i</sub></span> with
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">i ≤ r</span>, and since the cumulative decay only decreases, that
      is <span class="font-mono text-sm">exp(gc<sub>r</sub> − gc<sub>i</sub>)</span> with a
      <em>non-positive</em> exponent. Every factor lands in (0, 1]. Same numbers, zero overflow: the
      identical computation had a maximum error of 10⁻³, i.e. ordinary fp16 rounding.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ 9 -->
  <section id="hybrid" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">9. The hybrid</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Here the paper is refreshingly honest: pure linear attention still loses at exact copying and
      precise long-range retrieval, and no amount of gating fixes it, because a fixed-size state
      cannot hold an arbitrarily long verbatim span. So Kimi Linear does not try. It interleaves
      <b>three KDA layers to one full-attention layer</b>, uniformly, throughout the stack.
    </p>

    <div data-widget="hybrid-stack"></div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Three quarters of the layers stop keeping a KV cache, which is where the 75% memory reduction
      and the ~6× decoding throughput at 1M tokens come from. The paper's ablation puts 3:1 at the
      sweet spot: 7:1 trains to a similar loss but generalises worse, 1:1 matches on quality but costs
      more at inference, and 0:1 — pure full attention — is worse than both.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ 10 -->
  <section id="results" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">10. Does it hold up?</h2>
    <div class="p-4 rounded border bg-amber-50 text-amber-900">
      <b>Experiments in progress.</b> This section will carry my own runs on two RTX 3090s: the
      synthetic recall/copy/state-tracking tasks across all six architectures, the kernel benchmarks,
      and a small language-model pretraining sweep over the hybrid ratio. Results — including
      whatever fails to reproduce at this scale — go here.
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ 11 -->
  <section id="wrong" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">11. Four things I got wrong</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Reimplementing this was mostly a lesson in how many ways an experiment can look healthy while
      measuring nothing. All four of these produced plausible curves.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      <b>1. <span class="font-mono text-sm">inf × 0 = NaN</span>.</b> In the decay-weighted Gram matrix
      I computed the exponential first and masked the upper triangle afterwards. Above the diagonal the
      exponent is large and positive, so those entries overflowed to infinity — and infinity times a
      zero mask is not zero, it is NaN. Clamping the exponent before exponentiating fixes it, and the
      clamp is inert on every entry that survives the mask.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      <b>2. A zero bias that erases the memory.</b> The decay is
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">log α = −exp(A) · softplus(·)</span>. Initialising that
      projection's bias to zero gives <span class="font-mono text-sm">softplus(0) = 0.69</span>, and with
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">exp(A)</span> up to 16 that is a decay of
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">e⁻¹¹</span> <em>per token</em>. The state was being wiped between
      writing a fact and reading it. The loss sat at exactly <span class="font-mono text-sm">ln(vocab)</span> —
      not "learning slowly", but pinned at chance forever, because once the signal is destroyed there is
      no gradient left to tell the gate to stop destroying it.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      <b>3. The decay initialisation silently decides the result.</b> Even with Mamba's standard
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">dt ∈ [10⁻³, 10⁻¹]</span>, the same model scored <b>1.00 on MQAR at
      T = 512 and 0.37 at T = 1024</b>. Not capacity — at T = 1024 it still solved four pairs perfectly.
      Purely the initialisation: no channel started with a memory timescale long enough to reach the
      query. Anyone comparing gated architectures without scaling this to their context length is
      partly measuring who got a luckier init.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      <b>4. My baseline was crippled and I nearly believed it.</b> Every linear variant carries a short
      convolution, which hands it a "look at the previous token" operation for free. My softmax
      attention baseline did not have one, and it stalled at 0.32 on MQAR while KDA scored 1.00 — a
      result that would have read as "linear attention beats attention at recall", which is the
      opposite of true. Giving attention the same convolution took it straight to 1.00. The full grid:
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-4">
      <table class="w-full max-w-md text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead><tr class="border-b-2 border-gray-300 text-left">
          <th class="py-2 pr-6 font-semibold">RoPE</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-6 font-semibold">Short conv</th>
          <th class="py-2 font-semibold">MQAR accuracy</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody class="font-mono">
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200 bg-green-50"><td class="py-1.5 pr-6">yes</td><td class="pr-6">yes</td><td class="font-semibold">1.00</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-1.5 pr-6">no</td><td class="pr-6">yes</td><td>0.92</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-1.5 pr-6">yes</td><td class="pr-6">no</td><td>0.32</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-1.5 pr-6">no</td><td class="pr-6">no</td><td>0.34</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      The convolution mattered far more than the positional encoding — an induction head has to learn
      "the token after the match", and the convolution simply hands it that primitive.
    </p>
  </section>

